  "meta": {
    "title": "Guaymas Redux: Purple Bacteria at the Sulfide-Oil Overlap",
    "date": "2026-11-09",
    "lesson": "At Guaymas Basin, hydrothermal fluids laced with sulfide and thermogenic oil rise through a narrow depth band where faint far-red/infrared-shifted sunlight still penetrates. Purple sulfur bacteria here run anoxygenic photosynthesis: they need BOTH light (for energy) and sulfide (as electron donor) at once, so they are squeezed into the thin overlap zone between the photic surface and the rising chemical plume. This level sets the light gradient and the sulfide plume to overlap only in a narrow mid-column band, forcing the player to find and hold that band rather than simply swimming toward either resource.",
    "citation": "Guaymas Basin hydrothermal field, Gulf of California (~2000m); purple/green sulfur bacteria at the sulfide-light overlap.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Guaymas Basin hydrothermal vent field, purple sulfur bacteria living at the rare overlap of residual light and sulfide-rich vent fluid."
  },
